## Classification

- Type: CI / browser-harness restoration
- Target: `release/0.1.0-r2`
- Owner: SOL CI
- Parent quarantine: #277
- Canonical product behavior: #43
- Train: SOL CI, after #277 quarantine lands

## Purpose

Restore the narrow left-edge snap-preview/snap acceptance quarantined by #277 back into required r2 Specialist CI once the browser interaction boundary is deterministic.

The quarantined acceptance lives in:
- `test/e2e/plasmon-golden-path-left-snap.spec.ts`
- tags: `@r2-quarantine @issue-277`

## Required restoration proof

Before removing `@r2-quarantine`, demonstrate the exact acceptance from clean packaged GitHub-hosted Specialist environments with:

- 5 consecutive first-attempt PASS executions;
- no Playwright retry;
- no sleeps;
- no timeout inflation;
- no weakened geometry/state assertions;
- no test-only product hook;
- no product code change unless independent evidence proves a real Windowing defect.

The acceptance must continue to prove the real rendered titlebar pointer path, visible left snap preview, preview geometry, dragged-window usable-workspace bounds, and committed `data-window-snap="left"` state.

## Evidence to investigate

Post-merge release head `96e44f3de3ca4b7ea9ef591333d176f12eb1fcfb` produced Packaged Browser #1035 / run `31860716242` / job `94953529468` where the shared #43 left-preview assertion failed first attempt and passed immediate Playwright retry.

Prior evidence also includes Browser #995 and PR #253 Specialist executions showing intermittent failure at the same #43 browser boundary while dedicated snap/restoration tests could pass.

## Completion

- identify and repair the deterministic browser-harness root cause, or route to product ownership if product behavior is actually wrong;
- obtain the 5-run clean stability proof above;
- remove the #277 quarantine tag;
- keep the acceptance in the complete Specialist inventory;
- update quarantine documentation and canonical inventory guard;
- request `plasmon-code-reviewer` through GitHub's actual reviewer-request mechanism.
